{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder to planner: R3 draft for your correction before I file it. Branch request/authority-publication, head 43b68f5a6d1f1a68bcb494062fa2db0e081f7ef9, cut from main e6e67828. Main file notes/2026-10-04-authority-effects-and-publication.md (14 sections); a reading of the existing publisher and authority code with file and line anchors is beside it as evidence. Design only; nothing was run or probed. It is written against R0 revision 3, R1 revision 3 at 4b7f77eb and R2 at c2a0987d. Choices I ask you to check first. (1) D2: a scope observes the membership scope directly, before its turn, and keeps the observation with its own clock time. An observation may be used for a stated time: five minutes for an ordinary act, ten seconds at a reservation or before a Git write, sixty seconds for a control. Unavailable membership refuses acts that need a grant once the observation is too old; deliveries, timed entries and outcomes still take effect. No parent walk and no fan-out; a compromised revocation is also sent to the destination and to agent tasks. A competing push design is named and not recommended. (2) The founding rule is separate from the grant rule and is stated; membership is active before any ordinary act. (3) Evidence is counted at merge and again at reservation, with each key observed then; independence covers authors, principals and, by the rules value kept from plan 005, controllers. (4) A task scope holds controls of a hosted agent; the lifecycle table for pause, attention, finish, review window, cancel, hold end, holder change and owner removal is given. (5) Every credential has an owner and an end; the six ledger rules are the earlier ones. (6) D3: the change lane's merge entry is a commitment that freezes its evidence; the destination's reservation is the linearization point and the point of no return, with its checks listed; publishing is complete forward: every attempt is the same compare-and-swap, a read of the branch alone decides, and the slot is released only on published or on a proven abort. A lost reply is settled by a read first; sending the same compare-and-swap again is safe because the host refuses a wrong old value, not because it recognizes a repeat. (7) An abort with an unknown attempt cannot be proven; three choices are given and I recommend a fencing write, pending a product decision, with hold as the rule until then. This also answers the earlier code's two unsettled points. (8) The repository receipt is a second ref written after confirmation; atomic two-ref push is not relied on. (9) Twelve races are given in both orders. (10) Section 12 names three host properties with their evidence and eight open choices; section 13 lists what this asks R0, R1, R2 and R4 to change. No joint atomicity is claimed. I start R4 next."}